rable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd003-format.yaml (full SRD for format)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/format component description)\n      - pkg/sys/ (IsTerminal used by ColorEnabled)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/humansize.go\n        action: create\n        note: HumanSizeOpts struct and HumanSize function\n      - path: pkg/format/color.go\n        action: create\n        note: FileTypeColor, Reset, ColorEnabled, SetColorEnabled, ResetColorEnabled\n      - path: pkg/format/pad.go\n        action: create\n        note: PadRight, PadLeft, Columns functions\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Define HumanSizeOpts struct with Binary bool field. Implement HumanSize(bytes int64,\n          opts HumanSizeOpts) string. When Binary is true, use 1024-based units (K, M, G, T)\n          matching GNU coreutils -h output. When Binary is false, use 1000-based units (kB,\n          MB, GB, TB) matching GNU coreutils --si output. Format values to match GNU output\n          precision (e.g., \"4.0K\", \"1.2M\").\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Implement FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string returning the ANSI escape sequence\n          for the given file type (directory=blue, symlink=cyan, executable=green, etc.\n          matching default GNU LS_COLORS). Implement Reset() string returning the ANSI reset\n          sequence. Implement ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool that returns true when w is a\n          terminal (using pkg/sys.IsTerminal). Implement SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) and\n          ResetColorEnabled() for manual override of color state.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Implement PadRight(s string, width int) string that right-pads s with spaces to\n          the given width. Implement PadLeft(s string, width int) string that left-pads s\n          with spaces to the given width. If s is already wider than width, return s\n          unchanged.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Implement Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string that arranges entries\n          into columns fitting within termWidth, using the same column-filling algorithm as\n          GNU ls -C (fill columns top-to-bottom, then left-to-right). Each column is padded\n          to the width of its widest entry plus 2 spaces.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          The package is github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/format. It imports\n          pkg/sys for IsTerminal. It must not import any cmd/ package.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          ColorEnabled uses a package-level variable that can be overridden by SetColorEnabled.\n          ResetColorEnabled restores the auto-detect behavior based on IsTerminal.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          HumanSize output must match GNU coreutils formatting exactly -- values below 10\n          show one decimal place (e.g., \"4.0K\"), values at 10 or above show no decimal\n          for whole numbers.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/format/... compiles without errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go vet ./pkg/format/... reports no issues\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: HumanSize produces GNU-compatible output for both binary and SI modes\n      - id: AC4\n        text: FileTypeColor returns correct ANSI codes for directories, symlinks, executables\n      - id: AC5\n        text: PadRight and PadLeft pad correctly and handle strings wider than width\n      - id: AC6\n        text: Columns arranges entries into columns fitting the given terminal width\n\n- index: 3\n  title: \"pkg/format: formatting tests (srd003-format)\"\n  dependency: 2\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd003-format.yaml (full SRD for format)\n      - pkg/format/humansize.go (HumanSize implementation from task 2)\n      - pkg/format/color.go (color functions from task 2)\n      - pkg/format/pad.go (PadRight, PadLeft, Columns from task 2)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/humansize_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Tests for HumanSize with binary and SI modes\n      - path: pkg/format/color_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Tests for FileTypeColor, Reset, ColorEnabled\n      - path: pkg/format/pad_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Tests for PadRight, PadLeft, Columns\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Test HumanSize with binary mode (1024-based): 0 bytes, 512 bytes, 1024 (1.0K),\n          1536 (1.5K), 1048576 (1.0M), 1073741824 (1.0G). Test SI mode (1000-based): 1000\n          (1.0kB), 1500 (1.5kB), 1000000 (1.0MB). Verify output matches GNU coreutils\n          formatting.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Test FileTypeColor returns correct ANSI escape sequences for regular files,\n          directories, symlinks, and executables. Test Reset returns the ANSI reset\n          sequence. Test ColorEnabled returns false for non-terminal writers (e.g.,\n          bytes.Buffer). Test SetColorEnabled overrides auto-detection and\n          ResetColorEnabled restores it.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Test PadRight and PadLeft with strings shorter than, equal to, and longer than\n          the target width. Test Columns with varying entry counts and terminal widths,\n          verifying that entries fill columns top-to-bottom and that column widths\n          accommodate the widest entry.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          HumanSize tests use table-driven test cases comparing output against known GNU\n          coreutils output values.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          ColorEnabled tests use bytes.Buffer as a non-terminal writer to verify\n          auto-detection returns false without requiring a real terminal.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        t